# Metrics and statistical approaches

The performance metrics below are calculated at the pen level from raw
feed intake, body weight, and removal data collected throughout the
trial. Each metric is computed for both individual weekly periods and
cumulative combined periods to capture both acute responses to the
challenge and overall recovery patterns.

<br>

```{r metric-formulas, results='asis', echo=FALSE}
cat('
\\begingroup
\\small
### 1. Body Weight Gain (BWG)
\\[
BWG = \\frac{\\text{Pen weight period end}}{\\text{Number of animals period end}} - \\frac{\\text{Pen weight period start}}{\\text{Number of animals period start}}
\\]

### 2. Feed Conversion Ratio (FCR)
\\[
FCR = \\frac{\\text{Total feed consumed in a period}}{\\left(\\text{Total pen weight at the end of the period} + \\text{Weight of the removed animals}\\right) - \\text{Total pen weight at the start of the period}}
\\]

Note: FCR may be negative or very large (or infinite) when BWG is zero or
negative. In a challenge model this can occur legitimately during the acute
phase when pigs lose weight. **Negative FCR values are retained and reported**
because they reflect the biological reality of the challenge response; they
are explained in context rather than suppressed. The Gain:Feed ratio (GF,
see below) is provided alongside FCR as a complementary metric that remains
interpretable under negative gain.

### 3. Gain:Feed Ratio (GF)
\\[
GF = \\frac{\\left(\\text{Total pen weight at the end of the period} + \\text{Weight of the removed animals}\\right) - \\text{Total pen weight at the start of the period}}{\\text{Total feed consumed in a period}}
\\]

Unlike FCR, GF remains interpretable when pigs lose weight: a negative GF
indicates net weight loss per unit of feed consumed, which is a biologically
meaningful outcome in a challenge model.

### 4. Feed Intake
\\[
\\text{Feed intake} = \\text{Total feed consumed in a period (raw value)}
\\]

### 5. Average Daily Feed Intake (ADFI)
\\[
ADFI = \\frac{\\text{Total feed consumed in a period}}{\\left(\\text{Number of animals period end} \\times \\text{Total number of days in the period}\\right) + \\left(\\text{Sum of days each removed animal spent in the period}\\right)}
\\]

### 6. Average Daily Gain (ADG) — Pen Level
\\[
ADG = \\frac{\\left(\\text{Total pen weight period end} + \\text{Weight of the removed animals}\\right) - \\text{Total pen weight period start}}{\\left(\\text{Number of animals period end} \\times \\text{Total number of days in the period}\\right) + \\left(\\text{Sum of days each removed animal spent in the period}\\right)}
\\]

### 7. Average Daily Gain (ADG) — Individual Level
\\[
ADG = \\frac{\\text{Individual weight period end} - \\text{Individual weight period start}}{\\text{Total number of days in the period}}
\\]
\\endgroup
')
```

<br>

# Statistical model formulation

<br>

## Pen-level performance variables

The pen-level model includes treatment as a fixed effect, starting body
weight as a covariate to account for initial weight differences between
pens, and random effects for weight block and room to control for
systematic variation in the experimental design.

```{r pen-model-eq, results='asis'}
cat("
$$
y_{ijkl} = \\beta_0 + \\text{treatment}_{k} + \\beta_1 \\cdot \\text{start weight}_{ijkl} + u_{j} + v_{l} + \\epsilon_{ijkl}
$$

- \\( y_{ijkl} \\): The value for the \\( i \\)-th pen in the \\( k \\)-th treatment group within the \\( j \\)-th block and the \\( l \\)-th room.
- \\( \\beta_0 \\): The fixed intercept, representing the overall mean response.
- \\( \\text{treatment}_{k} \\): The fixed effect of the \\( k \\)-th treatment group, \\( k = [\\text{F18-ETEC (PC), No Chall (NC), 1BP (BP-L), 2BP (BP-SL)}] \\).
- \\( \\beta_1 \\): The fixed effect coefficient for \\( \\text{start weight} \\), a covariate adjusting for differences in initial weights.
- \\( u_{j} \\): The random effect associated with the \\( j \\)-th weight block, \\( j = [1,\\ldots,4] \\).
- \\( v_{l} \\): The random effect associated with the \\( l \\)-th room.
- \\( \\epsilon_{ijkl} \\): The random residual.
")
```

<br>

## Individual body weight

The individual body weight model extends the pen-level model by adding
animal nested within pen as a random effect, and sex as an additional
random effect. The treatment × day interaction captures how treatment
differences evolve over the course of the trial.

```{r ind-bw-model-eq, results='asis'}
cat("
$$
y_{ijklm} = \\beta_0 + \\text{treatment}_{k} + \\beta_1 \\cdot \\text{start weight}_{ijklm} + u_{j} + w_{ij} + s_{m} + v_{l} + \\epsilon_{ijklm}
$$

- \\( u_{j} \\): Random effect of the \\( j \\)-th weight block.
- \\( w_{ij} \\): Random effect of the \\( i \\)-th pen nested within the \\( j \\)-th block.
- \\( s_{m} \\): Random effect of the \\( m \\)-th sex.
- \\( v_{l} \\): Random effect of the \\( l \\)-th room.
")
```

<br>

## Pen-level CV (calculated from individual body weight)

```{r cv-model-eq, results='asis'}
cat("
$$
y_{ij} = \\beta_0 + \\text{treatment}_{k} + \\beta_1 \\cdot \\text{n animal}_{ij} + u_{j} + \\epsilon_{ij}
$$
")
```

<br>

## Categorical variables (fecal score, demeanor and abdominal score)

```{r cat-model-eq, results='asis'}
cat("
$$
y_{ijkl} = \\beta_0 + \\text{treatment}_k + \\beta_1 \\cdot \\text{day of trial}_{l} +
\\beta_2 (\\text{treatment}_k \\cdot \\text{day of trial}_{l}) + u_j + v_{j(i)} + w_m + \\epsilon_{ijkl}
$$
")
```

<br>

## Statistical notation

**Letters indicate significant differences (P < 0.05) between treatment
groups based on Dunnett's test, contrasting IVP treatments against the
F18-ETEC (PC) group. Groups sharing the same letter are not
significantly different from F18-ETEC (PC). No contrasts are made
between the IVP groups.**

**† indicates a tendency (0.05 < P < 0.1) between treatment groups.**

\*\*\* P < 0.001 | \*\* P < 0.01 | \* P < 0.05 | † P < 0.10

