# Performance: data analyses and results

## Body weight (kg): mean ± SD

```{r bw-summary-table, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, eval=TRUE}
summarize_data <- function(data, group_vars, summary_var) {
  data %>%
    group_by(across(all_of(group_vars))) %>%
    summarise(
      mean = round(mean(.data[[summary_var]], na.rm = TRUE), 2),
      sd  = round(sd(.data[[summary_var]],  na.rm = TRUE), 2),
      cov = round((sd / mean) * 100, 1),
      .groups = "drop"
    )
}

df_bw <- summarize_data(
  data    = feed_bw,
  group_vars = c("treatment_full_name", "day_of_trial"),
  summary_var = "bw_av"
) %>%
  pivot_longer(cols = c(mean, sd, cov), names_to = "metric", values_to = "value") %>%
  pivot_wider(names_from = treatment_full_name, values_from = value) %>%
  as.data.frame()

datatable_func(df_bw)

addWorksheet(wb_results, "raw_mean_body_weight")
writeData(wb_results, sheet = "raw_mean_body_weight", df_bw, colNames = TRUE)
```

The table above summarises pen-level average body weight by treatment
and day of trial. Pigs began the study with very similar body weights
across treatments, indicating successful randomisation. Over time, the
strict negative control maintained higher body weights than the
challenged positive control, while the IVP treatments showed
intermediate values, suggesting partial mitigation of the challenge
effect.

Mean body weight per pig was summarised by treatment and day of trial.
Error bands represent the standard error of the mean.

