# Pen-level CV analyses

The coefficient of variation (CV%) of individual body weights within
each pen is used as a measure of uniformity. A higher CV indicates
greater within-pen weight variability. The mixed model adjusts for
the number of animals per pen as a covariate. No statistical
differences were expected between treatments on any day of trial in this
metric; any significant results would suggest unequal variability as a
consequence of the challenge or treatment response.

<br>

```{r cv-analysis, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, echo=FALSE, eval=TRUE}
pen_bw_cv <- individual_bw %>%
  group_by(pen, day_of_trial) %>%
  reframe(n_animal = n(),
          mean_bw = mean(bw, na.rm = TRUE),
          sd_bw  = sd(bw,  na.rm = TRUE),
          cv_bw  = (sd_bw / mean_bw) * 100) %>%
  left_join(select(per_dat, treatment_full_name, weight_block, room, pen),
            by = "pen") %>%
  distinct() %>%
  as.data.frame()

addWorksheet(wb_raw_data, "pen_cv")
writeData(wb_raw_data, sheet = 6, pen_bw_cv, colNames = TRUE)

pen_bw_cv_wide <- pen_bw_cv %>%
  pivot_wider(names_from = "day_of_trial", values_from = "cv_bw") %>%
  rename_with(~ paste0("day_", .), .cols = matches("^\\d+$")) %>%
  filter(!is.na(treatment_full_name)) %>%
  as.data.frame()

formula_cv <- as.formula("Value ~ Treatment_ID + n_animal + (1|weight_block)")

